'''violone''' (vē-ọ-lō′ne), ''n.'' [= F. ''violon'' (dim.), a violin, < It. ''violone,'' aug. of ''viola,'' a viol: see ''viol.''] '''1.''' The largest of the medieval viols; a double-bass viol. {{smaller|It was originally a very large viola da gamba, sometimes provided with six strings, but usually with only three or four. The three-stringed form was tuned thus: G, D, A (the third below middle C), which is the tuning of the modern three-stringed double-bass, with which the violone is nearly identical.}}

'''2.''' In ''organ-building,'' a pedal stop of sixteen-feet tone, resembling the violoncello.

